<header class="page-hero">
  <div class="page-hero-inner">
    <span class="tag">{% t %}the second question{% endt %}</span>
    <h1 class="page-h1">{% t %}What to send{% endt %}</h1>
    <p class="page-lede">
      {% t %}Knowing who to call was never the bottleneck. The afternoon goes on the
      deck, the emails, the proposal and the call sheet — written in your voice,
      about their business, with nothing made up. Prospektor drafts all of it in
      the same run that found them, and shows you where every claim came from.
      Whether you are asking them to buy, to resell you, or to invest.{% endt %}
    </p>
    <div class="page-hero-actions">
      <a class="btn-cta" href="{{ '/pricing/' | localize }}">{% t %}See the price{% endt %}</a>
      <a class="btn-ghost" href="{{ '/who-to-pitch/' | localize }}">{% t %}← First: who to pitch{% endt %}</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</header>

<section class="deliverables">
  <div class="deliverables-inner">
    <h2 class="sec-mid">{% t %}Out of one run.{% endt %}</h2>
    <ul class="card-grid">
      <li class="card is-static"><div class="card-body">
        <span class="card-topic">{% t %}deck{% endt %}</span>
        <h3 class="card-title">{% t %}Pitch deck{% endt %}</h3>
        <p class="card-dek">{% t %}Their business, your case, in your structure. Branded, editable, printable.{% endt %}</p>
      </div></li>
      <li class="card is-static"><div class="card-body">
        <span class="card-topic">{% t %}leave-behind{% endt %}</span>
        <h3 class="card-title">{% t %}One-pager{% endt %}</h3>
        <p class="card-dek">{% t %}The version that survives being forwarded to somebody who wasn't in the room.{% endt %}</p>
      </div></li>
      <li class="card is-static"><div class="card-body">
        <span class="card-topic">{% t %}outreach{% endt %}</span>
        <h3 class="card-title">{% t %}Cold-email sequence{% endt %}</h3>
        <p class="card-dek">{% t %}Not one email. The sequence, each one earning the next.{% endt %}</p>
      </div></li>
      <li class="card is-static"><div class="card-body">
        <span class="card-topic">{% t %}paper{% endt %}</span>
        <h3 class="card-title">{% t %}Written proposal{% endt %}</h3>
        <p class="card-dek">{% t %}The long form, for when they ask you to put it in writing.{% endt %}</p>
      </div></li>
      <li class="card is-static"><div class="card-body">
        <span class="card-topic">{% t %}the call{% endt %}</span>
        <h3 class="card-title">{% t %}Call sheet{% endt %}</h3>
        <p class="card-dek">{% t %}What they will object to, and what you say back. Before the meeting, not after.{% endt %}</p>
      </div></li>
      <li class="card is-static"><div class="card-body">
        <span class="card-topic">{% t %}warm{% endt %}</span>
        <h3 class="card-title">{% t %}Intro request{% endt %}</h3>
        <p class="card-dek">{% t %}The message to the person who can open the door, written for them to forward.{% endt %}</p>
      </div></li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="flow">
  <div class="flow-inner">

    <article class="flowstep">
      <span class="flowstep-n">01</span>
      <div class="flowstep-body">
        <h2>{% t %}Your voice — learned from what you actually sent.{% endt %}</h2>
        <p>
          {% t %}Edit a draft in the studio and save it. That edit becomes a brand-voice
          example, and future runs are written against it — tone and structure
          only, never the facts. A run picks up to eight of them, and it picks
          them <em>for this target</em>: the saved edits whose subject looks most
          like the company being pitched, with at least one example of each
          deliverable it has to write.{% endt %}
        </p>
        <p class="flowstep-proof">{% t %}Nobody has to describe their tone of voice. You just edit, and it learns.{% endt %}</p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="flowstep">
      <span class="flowstep-n">02</span>
      <div class="flowstep-body">
        <h2>{% t %}Grounded in what has already worked for you.{% endt %}</h2>
        <p>
          {% t %}Record what happened — the meeting that landed, the objection that
          killed it, the deal that closed — and it is folded into every run after
          it. A pitch is not a fresh guess each time: it is written against your
          own outcomes, your proof points and your banned terms.{% endt %}
        </p>
        <p>
          {% t %}Which means run one hundred is better than run one, and the workspace
          that writes down what happened is the workspace that pulls ahead. This
          is the single highest-leverage thing a team does in here.{% endt %}
        </p>
        <p class="flowstep-proof">{% t %}The studio gets better the more your team uses it. That is the whole design.{% endt %}</p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="flowstep">
      <span class="flowstep-n">03</span>
      <div class="flowstep-body">
        <h2>{% t %}No invented numbers. Ever.{% endt %}</h2>
        <p>
          {% t %}Numbers come from your brief or from a cited source. Anything else
          becomes a bracketed placeholder and is listed for you to fill — so a
          gap looks like a gap instead of a confident sentence you have to
          fact-check under time pressure.{% endt %}
        </p>
        <p>
          {% t %}Then it reads its own draft back before you ever see it: banned terms,
          unsourced claims, and any mention of a company the identity phase had
          already ruled out. What survives that read-back is what lands in your
          tab.{% endt %}
        </p>
        <p class="flowstep-proof">{% t %}Every claim carries what was read. Provenance is never withheld.{% endt %}</p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="flowstep">
      <span class="flowstep-n">04</span>
      <div class="flowstep-body">
        <h2>{% t %}It is a draft, and you are still the closer.{% endt %}</h2>
        <p>
          {% t %}Every block is copyable and pencil-editable in place. Ask the studio
          where a claim came from, how to open the first call, what to say when
          the predicted objection lands — grounded in that pitch and your brief,
          and deliberately read-only, so editing stays with you.{% endt %}
        </p>
        <p>
          {% t %}Share a pitch with a link, print it, or hand it to a colleague. The
          edits you save on the way out are the same edits that teach the next
          run.{% endt %}
        </p>
        <p class="flowstep-proof">{% t %}Minutes to a first draft. The judgement stays yours.{% endt %}</p>
      </div>
    </article>

  </div>
</section>

<section class="page-split">
  <div class="page-split-inner">
    <span class="tag">{% t %}try it{% endt %}</span>
    <h2>{% t %}Free tells you who to chase. Paid gives you what to send.{% endt %}</h2>
    <p>
      {% t %}The scan is free and always will be — point it at your own site and see who
      it would hunt for you. Everything on this page starts when you do.{% endt %}
    </p>
    <a class="btn-cta" href="{{ '/pricing/' | localize }}">{% t %}One price, the whole box →{% endt %}</a>
  </div>
</section>
